'' A BYREF RESULT THAT IS 'this' IS THE RECEIVER POINTER'S VALUE. THIS holds a pointer,
'' so the object's address is what the slot CONTAINS -- but the result assignment took
'' the ADDRESS of the expression, so 'function = this' emitted 'store @$tmp0, @this':
'' a pointer to a pointer returned where the declaration promises a pointer to the
'' object. The caller then took a field offset off it and loaded garbage, and the whole
'' thing lowered and verified. ReceiverOf already applies the rule for an unqualified
'' sibling call; the result path did not.
''
'' Measured -- fbc's C for all three spellings is the same one line:
''
''   struct $1W* _ZN1W5SELF_Ev( struct $1W* THIS$1 )
''   { struct $1W* fb$result$1; fb$result$1 = THIS$1; return fb$result$1; }
''
'' 'function = this', 'return this', and an 'operator []( ... ) byref as W' returning
'' this -- which is how overload/op-ptrindex.bas writes its byrefResult group.
''
'' Asserted as the ABSENCE of ', @this' rather than against a golden: every legitimate
'' use of the receiver in this file is the pointer's VALUE, so the slot's own address
'' appearing anywhere as an operand is the bug itself.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/byref-result-this.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-NOT: , @this
